## How to Choose a GEO Agency

To compare GEO providers, request a repeatable record for the platforms and buyer questions that matter to your business. A dated screenshot can show one appearance; it cannot establish persistent visibility or prove the provider caused it. Record the prompt, platform/mode, market and date, then distinguish a linked URL citation, brand mention, referral session and qualified enquiry. This guide has not run that controlled comparison across the agencies listed.

1. **Evidence for the promised outcome.** For citation work, ask for the cited URL and captured answer. For lead generation, ask how a recorded enquiry was attributed and qualified. A brand name in an answer is a mention unless a supporting URL is linked.
2. **Entity authority capability.** Understanding of how LLMs identify and trust entities. See [what is entity SEO](/blog/what-is-entity-seo/).
3. **Technical and content eligibility.** Ask how the provider checks crawl access, indexation, snippet controls and factual content. Structured data should match the visible page; Google does not require special AI markup.
4. **A measurement method.** How they track citations and generative-engine visibility over time, not just rankings.
5. **Published methodology, not slogans.** Evidence of *how* they work, weighted above testimonials.

If your brand is missing from generative answers, start with [why your brand is not appearing in ChatGPT](/blog/brand-not-appearing-in-chatgpt/), then build the foundation with [how to build topical authority](/blog/how-to-build-topical-authority/). The discipline that applies to any SEO hire is set out in [how to choose an SEO consultant](/blog/how-to-choose-seo-consultant/).

---

## Questions to Ask Any GEO Provider

1. **"Show me a brand you have made appear in a generative answer."** A screenshot from an AI Overview, ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Copilot. No example means no proof.
2. **"How do you build and measure entity authority?"** A real answer references corroboration, structured data, and citation tracking, not just "we optimise for AI".
3. **"What work does the GEO fee cover?"** Compare the scope, implementation and measurement whether GEO is a separate project or part of an SEO retainer. Ask for evidence matching the promised outcome.
4. **"What changed in your method over the last six months?"** Generative search moves fast. A provider who cannot answer is not keeping up.
5. **"What do I own if we stop after three months?"** You should keep all strategy, audits, and content.

---

## How this comparison was assembled

The September 2026 research reviewed four types of published information. No standardised performance score or controlled provider comparison was run:

1. **A genuine GEO or AI search service page**, naming Generative Engine Optimisation, AI search, or the specific generative engines (AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Copilot) explicitly, rather than an "AI" mention buried in a broader SEO page.
2. **Published case studies or client evidence**, checked directly on the agency's own site, favouring any result tied specifically to an AI Overview, citation, or generative-engine outcome over a generic traffic or ranking number.
3. **My own enquiry evidence**, disclosed separately: the April 2026 Express Medicals inbound enquiry after a Bing Copilot citation. The enquiry did not become a paid engagement. It supports the discovery example in the owned-service profile, not a claim that competitors lack enquiries.
4. **Pricing transparency**, noting plainly where a figure is published and where it is not, rather than presenting an estimate as fact.

The original September research checked the cited service pages and case studies directly; they were not all rechecked for this October disclosure correction. Several sites claiming "GEO agency" services for individual UK towns, including some appearing prominently in search results for Edinburgh, Exeter, and Leicester, were checked and excluded because they could not be verified as a real local team or office at the time of writing.

**Provider-source review: 10 September 2026. Disclosure and comparison limits corrected: 2 October 2026.**

